<tr valign="top">

<td>
    <a href="/pub/eswp/speciesSummary.do?id=13943" style="valign:middle">AFBAA02120</a></td>

<td><i>Entosphenus macrostoma</i></td>

<td>
<i>Lampetra macrostoma</i></td>

<td>Cowichan Lake Lamprey</td>

<td>
  Lake Lamprey; Vancouver Island Lake Lamprey; Vancouver Lamprey</td>

<td>Robins, C.R., R.M. Bailey, C.E. Bond, J.R. Brooker, E.A. Lachner, R.N. Lea, and W.B. Scott. 1991. Common and scientific names of fishes from the United States and Canada. American Fisheries Society, Special Publication 20. 183 pp.</td>
<td>Species</td>
<td>
    April 11, 2013 - Changed from <i>Lampetra macrostoma</i> to <i>Entosphenus macrostomus</i> to align with NatureServe (DDW).<br/>Aug. 2025 - the National Scientific Name changed from Entosphenus macrostomus to Entosphenus macrostoma (Page et al. 2023)<br/><br/>There are a number of freshwater populations derived from L. TRIDENTATA along the south coast of British Columbia. At one time one occurred in Elsie Lake near Port Alberni, and a similar parasitic form exists today in Ruby, Hotel and Sakinaw lakes on the Sechelt Peninsula (P91MCP01BCCA, O91UBC01BCCA). There is also a landlocked predaceous lamprey in Village Lake on Quadra Island. In these cases, however, there is no contact with seagoing lamprey so there is no evidence of biological isolation separating them as species. We are tracking only those populations which are separated by biological, rather than physical barriers.</td>
