<tr valign="top">

<td>
    <a href="/pub/eswp/speciesSummary.do?id=16839" style="valign:middle">AFCJB16020</a></td>

<td><i>Hybognathus hankinsoni</i></td>

<td>
</td>

<td>Brassy Minnow</td>

<td>
  </td>

<td>Robins, C.R., R.M. Bailey, C.E. Bond, J.R. Brooker, E.A. Lachner, R.N. Lea, and W.B. Scott. 1991. Common and scientific names of fishes from the United States and Canada. American Fisheries Society, Special Publication 20. 183 pp.</td>
<td>Species</td>
<td>
    </td>
<td>Vertebrate Animal</td>
<td>F-HYHA</td>
<td>ray-finned fishes</td>
<td>Animalia</td>
<td>Craniata</td>
<td>Actinopterygii</td>
<td>Cypriniformes</td>
<td>Leuciscidae</td>
<td>

    </td>
<td>G5</td>
<td>Aug 2015</td>
<td>S4</td>
<td>Aug 2011</td>
<td>Jan 2004</td>
<td>No Status</td>
<td> </td>
<td></td>
<td></td>
<td>
  </td>
<td></td>
<td> </td>
<td></td>
<td>
</td>

<td></td>

<td></td>

<td>4 - Secure(2005)</td>

<td></td>

<td></td>
<td></td>
<td>
</td>



<td>Native</td>
<td>Regularly occurring</td>
<td></td>
<td>N</td>
<td>N</td>
<td></td>

<td>
    </td>

    <td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>
    </td>

<td>
    Habitat includes small, clear, sluggish weedy creeks or small rivers with sand, gravel, or mud bottom overlain with organic sediment; this species also is common in cool, stained or acid waters of boggy streams, ponds, and lakes, and it is common in overflow ponds near rivers. Spawning occurs among vegetation in shallows and in flooded marshes. Eggs are scattered on vegetation and on the bottom (Becker 1983). </td>

    <td>
      </td>

<td>
 </td>

<td></td>

<td></td>

<td></td>

<td></td>

<td></td>

<td>
Spawns in spring. Eggs hatch in a week to 10 days. Sexually mature at age 1-2 (Becker 1983). </td>

<td>
  </td>

<td>
A schooling species (Becker 1983). </td>

<td>
  </td>

<td></td>

<td></td>


<td>
Herbivore: Adult, Immature; Invertivore: Adult, Immature</td>


<td>Eats algae, phyto- and zooplankton, benthic invertebrates, surface drift, bottom ooze (Becker 1983).</td>

<td></td>

<td>
</td>

<td>Winters in pools and deep runs under shelter or buried in debris (Becker 1983).</td>

<td></td>
    
<td></td>

<td></td>

<td>
    </td>

<td>
</td>

<td></td>

<td></td>

<td>Range extends from the St. Lawrence River and Lake Champlain drainages, Quebec and Vermont, across the Great Lakes, Hudson Bay, and Missouri-upper Mississippi river basins of southern Canada and northern U.S. south to Colorado and Missouri (Scheurer et al. 2003); MacKenzie River system (Arctic Basin), Alberta; Fraser River system (Pacific Slope), British Columbia (Page and Burr 2011).</td>

<td>
</td>

<td>
</td>

<td></td>

<td></td>

<td></td>

<td>
    </td>


<td>Hammerson, G.</td>

<td>Feb 14, 2012</td>

<td>CANNINGS, S.G.</td>

<td>Jan 23, 1992</td>

</tr>

